WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.260
Hey, it's Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.620
And this is Tesla Short's time daily episode 435.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.580
It's April 13th, 2026.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.420
And we've got a full slate of stories

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.680
that feel like they're all pulling

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.500
at different corners of the same company.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.200
One foot firmly in Europe trying to prove it belongs.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.400
The other foot back home figuring out

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.020
what it wants to be when it grows up.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:19.080
Let's jump in.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:21.900
Tesla finally cleared its first regulatory hurdle

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:23.740
for FSD supervised in Europe.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.060
And the country that said yes is the Netherlands.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.740
After roughly four years of quiet lobbying,

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.020
data sharing, and what I can only imagine

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.040
were some very patient meetings in Brussels

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.760
and the Hague regulators there have signed off.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.260
It's a genuine milestone,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.540
but it lands with a list of caveats

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.460
that tell you a lot about how Europe

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.820
is choosing to walk into this technology.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.940
Compared to what we see in North America,

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:48.960
the Dutch version arrives with tighter geofencing,

00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.040
more conservative speed profiles in certain zones,

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.880
and an even stronger emphasis

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.460
that a human must remain fully attentive.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.600
That's not exactly a surprise given how risk averse

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.660
European safety regulators have been,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:04.420
but it does mean Tesla is adapting its stack

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:06.600
rather than simply exporting the version

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.080
that's been running on California and Texas roads.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:11.460
From a business angle, this is still important

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.480
because every supervised mile driven

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.940
on European pavement feeds the improvement loop.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.720
More diverse weather, narrower streets,

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.480
different signage, different driver behavior,

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.520
all of it becomes training data

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.380
that eventually makes the system smarter everywhere.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:29.680
The approval also feels like a quiet acknowledgement

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.740
that Tesla has been willing to dial back

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.300
the marketing language and meet regulators where they are.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:37.420
Instead of fighting for unsupervised claims

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:38.400
right out of the gate,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:41.060
the company is letting supervised operation act

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:41.940
as the on ramp.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.720
That measured approach might frustrate some owners

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.800
who want full hands-off freedom tomorrow,

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.060
but it could actually smooth the path

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.780
for other EU countries that have been watching closely.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:53.880
Germany, France, the Nordics,

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:56.020
they all have their own safety bureaucracies

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.680
that take cues from one another.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:01:59.800
A cautious green light in the Netherlands

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.080
could become the template rather than the exception.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.780
One practical wrinkle that's already causing real world friction.

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.500
The approval is limited to vehicles with HW-4 hardware.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:13.020
Owners of older HW-3 cars in the Netherlands

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.620
have been told they're not eligible

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.720
and at least one person has already been offered a refund

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.060
after buying an anticipation of the rollout.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:21.000
That stings.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.340
It's a tangible reminder that the gap between

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.640
hardware generations isn't just about performance anymore.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.100
It's starting to affect regulatory eligibility

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:29.800
in new markets.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:33.660
Tesla has a huge installed base of HW-3 vehicles

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.940
across Europe and how they choose to support

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.360
or eventually upgrade those cars will say a lot

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:40.880
about how the company treats its early customers

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:42.600
as the autonomy story matures.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:45.980
It also quietly underscores that the performance difference

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:48.460
between the two generations has grown large enough

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:50.160
for regulators to draw a hard line.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.940
While Europe cautiously cracks the door open,

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.380
Tesla is simultaneously closing a chapter on its home turf.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.740
The company has ended regular production

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.720
of both the Model S and Model X.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.540
What's left is a final invite only run

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.320
of 250 signature addition vehicles

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.040
each carrying a sticker price around $160,000.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.100
These cars feel like a valedictory lap

00:03:13.100 --> 00:03:16.200
for the pair of flagships that essentially launch Tesla

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.320
as a serious luxury brand back in the early 2000s.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:21.600
Think about it, the Model S was the car

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.000
that convinced a lot of skeptics and electric sedan

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:26.860
could be faster, quieter, and more advanced

00:03:26.860 --> 00:03:28.480
than anything coming out of Germany.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.800
The Model X brought the Falcon Wing doors

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:33.440
and that massive windshield that still turns heads today.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.240
They were halo products in the truest sense,

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.260
expensive, ambitious, and they carried the weight

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.440
of proving the entire company wasn't just a flash in the pan.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:45.520
Now, after more than a decade, their time

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:47.080
in regular production is over.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.240
The signature additions will likely become collector pieces

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.860
and I suspect we'll see them pop up at auctions

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:56.280
in 10 or 15 years the way early Porsche 911s

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.080
or original land rovers do today.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.760
For loyal owners who've stuck with these cars

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:02.520
through multiple refreshes, it creates

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:03.880
a nice bit of exclusivity.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.400
For Tesla, the move is purely strategic.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:09.800
It frees up engineering bandwidth, manufacturing lines,

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:13.140
and supply chain focus for the next generation platforms

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.820
that are supposed to drive the bulk of future volume.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.120
That pivot toward volume and affordability

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:20.980
is showing up in other concrete ways.

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:24.240
There are consistent reports that Tesla is developing

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.180
a more accessible SUV that would sit below

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:28.480
the current Model Y.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.460
The idea has been floating around for years.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:35.840
Elon has talked about vehicles in the $25 to $30,000 range

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.500
once the company can hit the necessary cost targets

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.080
through manufacturing improvements.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:43.780
This new entry level SUV would apparently lean heavily

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.800
on the next generation platform

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:49.400
and the structural battery techniques Tesla has been perfecting.

00:04:49.700 --> 00:04:51.900
If they can actually deliver something

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.260
in that price bracket with decent range

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.580
and the build quality owners have come to expect,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:59.820
it changes the math on who can even consider a Tesla.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:02.940
Markets like parts of Europe, Southeast Asia

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.660
and even more price sensitive pockets of North America

00:05:05.660 --> 00:05:06.860
suddenly open up.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:09.680
It's the logical next step after years of pushing

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:10.900
the technology frontier.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:13.720
Now the company needs to broaden the customer base

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.020
so the fixed costs of autonomy development

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.160
can be spread across millions more vehicles.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.880
It also signals that the Halo car era

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.520
we just talked about with the SNX

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:24.460
is giving way to something more democratic.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:27.660
That's a healthy evolution for any company

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.840
that wants to move from niche innovator to mainstream player

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.960
though it does mean saying goodbye to some of the romance

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.340
that came with those original flagship models.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:39.460
Of course moving down market isn't without its bumps.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.760
The cyber truck Tesla's most expensive vehicle right now

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.560
just recorded what looks like a record low sales week

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:46.580
in the United States.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.500
The numbers are soft enough that they've caught attention

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:50.780
but the context matters.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.700
A lot of the early adopter wave that was willing

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:56.740
to pay the six figure prices for the foundation series

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.380
seems to have satisfied its curiosity.

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.340
Many prospective buyers are openly saying

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.340
they're waiting for the more affordable variant

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:05.860
that's expected later this year,

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:07.680
the one that's supposed to bring the truck

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.120
into more realistic daily driver territory.

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:13.420
This law feels familiar if you've followed Tesla

00:06:13.420 --> 00:06:14.540
for any length of time.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.260
We saw the same pattern with the Model 3,

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.260
strong initial reservation wave,

00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:22.360
then a noticeable pause until the standard range versions

00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:24.840
and price cuts brought in the next surge.

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:28.600
The cyber truck's current pricing puts it in rarefied air

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.680
and for a vehicle with its size and shape

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.800
that limits the addressable audience.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:37.460
Once a sub $70,000 version arrives and production scales,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:40.540
the expectation is that demand should rebound sharply.

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.540
Still, the softness is a useful reality check.

00:06:43.660 --> 00:06:45.360
It reminds everyone that even a product

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:47.440
with as much built in hype as the cyber truck

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.360
eventually has to prove itself on price, practicality

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:51.600
and total cost of ownership.

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.460
Tesla will be watching these numbers closely

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:56.140
because the truck is supposed to be one

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.160
of the higher margin contributors in the lineup.

00:06:58.760 --> 00:07:01.500
Closer to home, some Canadian model Y performance owners

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.800
just got an unwelcome surprise with their new cars.

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.700
The refreshed performance trim at least in Canada

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:09.200
appears to be missing vehicle to load capability,

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:11.600
the ability to power tools, appliances

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:14.540
or even an entire campsite from the car's battery.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.060
That feature is present on other variants

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:18.540
of the refreshed Model Y

00:07:18.540 --> 00:07:20.940
and on the performance model in other markets

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:23.760
which has left owners here trading theories on forms.

00:07:24.200 --> 00:07:26.580
Is it a software lockout, a hardware difference

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:29.100
specific to the performance motor configuration,

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:32.960
a regional certification quirk tied to cold weather testing,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:35.440
Tesla hasn't issued a clear statement yet

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:37.840
and the silence is only feeding the speculation.

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:39.920
For people who bought the performance

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.060
expecting it to be the most capable version

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.160
in every respect, the stings.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.680
In Canada, especially where cottage country power outages

00:07:47.680 --> 00:07:49.860
are a regular summer and winter occurrence,

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:51.900
being able to run a fridge, lights

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.680
or medical equipment from your car isn't a gimmick.

00:07:54.920 --> 00:07:56.280
It's practical piece of mind.

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:59.060
The episode is a small one in the grand scheme

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.300
but it illustrates how even a company

00:08:01.300 --> 00:08:03.180
is vertically integrated as Tesla

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:05.820
can still have regional product decisions

00:08:05.820 --> 00:08:07.580
that feel arbitrary to buyers.

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.820
It's the kind of detail that loyal customers notice

00:08:10.820 --> 00:08:13.600
and remember and it puts pressure on Tesla's product

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:15.660
and regional teams to communicate better

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.060
when these discrepancies appear.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:20.360
On a more serious note, there's an ongoing investigation

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.300
in Norway that just got considerably more complicated.

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.340
A critical network communications module

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.820
was stolen from a model Y that was involved

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.540
in a violent 2023 crash in Bergen.

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:34.680
The car had accelerated dramatically off the road

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:37.280
under circumstances that are still not fully understood.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.740
Investigators had hoped to pull telemetry and camera data

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.120
that hadn't yet been uploaded to Tesla's servers

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:45.900
but the missing module severs that direct link.

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:49.920
Without it, some of the most granular evidence is now gone

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:51.800
or at least much harder to recover.

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.500
This case stands out because it touches

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.460
on several sensitive issues at once.

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.540
The trustworthiness of crash data,

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:00.580
the physical security of those logging systems

00:09:00.580 --> 00:09:02.880
and the growing global scrutiny around advanced driver

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:04.480
assistance and autonomy features.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:06.740
It's a reminder that no matter how sophisticated

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:09.080
the over-the-air telemetry is,

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:11.660
the physical hardware still has to be tamper resistant,

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:13.980
especially at the scene of a serious incident.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.080
For Tesla, it adds another data point to the argument

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:20.680
that they need robust, perhaps even redundant,

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:23.380
ways to protect and retrieve that information,

00:09:23.780 --> 00:09:25.620
even when law enforcement or third parties

00:09:25.620 --> 00:09:27.200
have custody of the vehicle.

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.400
The situation is uncomfortable, but it's also relatively rare.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.100
Most crashes still yield clear telemetry

00:09:33.100 --> 00:09:34.620
because the modules do their job.

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.420
Still, one high-profile failure like this

00:09:37.420 --> 00:09:40.480
can erode confidence if it isn't addressed transparently.

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:43.000
Shifting from regulatory headaches

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:44.200
to something more optimistic,

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.720
Tesla is bringing the semi-north of the border

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:47.800
in a more public way.

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:50.420
The electric class eight truck is scheduled

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:52.020
to appear at the truck world show,

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.820
and it follows a private event at the new Vaughan

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:55.880
Ontario showroom.

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:59.160
For Canadian fleet operators, this is a big deal.

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:02.000
Long haul routes in this country often involve

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.580
serious temperature swings, highway speeds

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:07.140
and payload weights that test any powertrain.

00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:08.880
Seeing the semi-up close,

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:10.640
talking to Tesla's commercial team

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:13.160
and getting real data on winter performance

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.840
and charging infrastructure will help fleets decide

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:17.580
whether electric heavy-duty trucks

00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:19.020
can fit into their operations.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:21.840
It also signals that Tesla isn't confining

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.060
its commercial ambitions to passenger cars.

00:10:24.800 --> 00:10:27.700
The semi has been in limited production for a while now,

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:29.420
and every public showing like this

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.260
moves it from prototype curiosity

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:34.160
towards something fleets can actually spec.

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:37.680
If the truck can prove itself on total cost of ownership,

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:41.020
lower fuel, lower maintenance and decent uptime,

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.900
it could accelerate the shift toward electrification

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:46.500
in one of the hardest parts of the transportation sector.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:48.660
We're still early, but the momentum feels real.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.360
Meanwhile, Wall Street is keeping its near-term expectations

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:51.920
in check.

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:54.520
RBC Capital recently lowered its price targets

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:56.240
on Tesla along with Lucid and Ford,

00:10:56.740 --> 00:10:58.600
citing softening EV demand signals

00:10:58.600 --> 00:10:59.900
and preparing for upcoming earnings.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:02.720
The adjustment isn't shocking given the recent delivery miss

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.660
and the uncertainty around exactly when

00:11:04.660 --> 00:11:07.240
those more affordable models will hit the market in volume.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.920
It's a classic reminder that analysts are paid

00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:11.320
to look at the next few quarters,

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:13.440
not the 10-year technology roadmap.

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.620
Tesla has faced these waves of skepticism before

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:18.360
and the stock has its own rhythm.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:20.740
Still, the caution from one of the bigger banks

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.200
does reflect a broader mood.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:25.200
Investors want to see execution on cost reduction

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:27.840
and new product timing before they'll fully reengage.

00:11:28.580 --> 00:11:30.340
One story I keep coming back to this week

00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:32.300
is that missing network card in Norway.

00:11:32.620 --> 00:11:35.000
It's easy to treat it as an isolated incident,

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:36.840
but it touches on something fundamental

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:39.480
about how we build trust in autonomous systems.

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:41.700
When something goes wrong at highway speed,

00:11:41.900 --> 00:11:43.540
the public and regulators need to believe

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:46.180
the data trail is intact and tamperproof.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:49.000
Tesla's long-term credibility on safety

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.080
partly rests on proving that its vehicles

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.320
are not black boxes,

00:11:52.840 --> 00:11:54.980
that the evidence is accessible and reliable.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:57.900
The best response is probably a combination

00:11:57.900 --> 00:12:00.320
of better physical security on those modules,

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:03.160
clearer hand-off protocols with law enforcement

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:06.020
and perhaps even redundant logging paths.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:08.380
It's the kind of unsexy engineering work

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:09.340
that doesn't make headlines

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:11.480
until something like this happens, but it matters.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.720
Stepping back from the day-to-day news,

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:16.200
it's worth applying a bit of first principles thinking

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.800
to the battery chemistry debate

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:19.220
that never seems to go away.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:21.000
You'll often hear blanket statements

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:24.080
that LFP batteries are universally cheaper and safer,

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.020
but that oversimplifies things

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:28.320
once you start looking at real-world duty cycles,

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:30.000
especially in cold climates.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.780
Physics doesn't care about marketing slogans.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:35.300
The practical question is where the trade-offs

00:12:35.300 --> 00:12:38.080
in energy density, weight, cold weather performance

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:41.240
and longevity make one chemistry the clearer winner

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.540
for a specific vehicle and geography.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:47.660
Early lithium ion cells delivered roughly 150 watt hours

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:48.320
per kilogram.

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:52.420
Modern NMC packs now routinely exceed 260

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:56.640
while LFP chemistries tend to land between 160 and 180.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:12:58.740
That gap adds up fast.

00:12:59.300 --> 00:13:03.440
On a large SUV or truck trying to deliver 500 kilometers

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:06.640
of real-world range in a Canadian or Nordic winter,

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:09.780
the LFP pack ends up significantly heavier.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:14.020
That extra mass hurts efficiency, acceleration, handling.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:17.280
And yes, it can even affect how much energy you need

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:18.360
just to move the thing.

00:13:18.840 --> 00:13:21.820
LFP also tends to suffer more at very low temperatures,

00:13:22.160 --> 00:13:25.280
which can mean bigger packs or more frequent charging stops

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:27.580
precisely when drivers want fewer of them.

00:13:28.080 --> 00:13:30.060
Tesla has taken the pragmatic route here.

00:13:30.420 --> 00:13:32.520
They treat chemistry as one variable inside

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:35.560
a much larger system that includes structural battery

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:38.860
integration, active thermal management, vehicle software

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:41.100
that can dynamically adjust charge profiles

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:43.460
and even the shape of the vehicle itself.

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.620
That's why you see different packs in different models

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:49.740
and different regions rather than an all or nothing bet

00:13:49.740 --> 00:13:50.980
on one cell type.

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:54.320
It feels like the mature engineering approach.

00:13:54.540 --> 00:13:57.660
Let the use case and the customer's actual needs dictate

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:00.040
the mix instead of declaring a single winner

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:01.260
for the entire industry.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.580
Over time, the chemistry that delivers the best balance

00:14:04.580 --> 00:14:07.900
of cost, range, longevity, safety, and cold weather

00:14:07.900 --> 00:14:09.980
behavior for each specific duty cycle

00:14:10.540 --> 00:14:12.220
is the one that will win in that segment.

00:14:12.660 --> 00:14:15.300
Tesla's willingness to run multiple chemistries in parallel

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:17.880
might end up being one of their quiet advantages.

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:20.500
Before we wrap up, it'll be interesting to watch

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:23.480
how European regulators react to the first batches

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:27.620
of real-world data coming out of those supervised Dutch miles.

00:14:28.240 --> 00:14:30.560
Early feedback there could either accelerate approvals

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.980
in neighboring countries or add a few more hurdles.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:36.320
Either way, the Netherlands just became a meaningful test bed.

